  "verdict": {
   "evidence": "strong",
   "headline": "Case closed: precipitation is rising.",
   "what": "Precipitation is rising.",
   "where": "Niamey, Niger, 1981–2025.",
   "how_much": "+0.0814 mm/day per decade (Sen's slope); the fitted line changes by +0.422 mm/day over the whole period (+29%).",
   "how_sure": "Both tests agree this is very unlikely to be chance (t-test p = 0.0011, Mann-Kendall corrected for autocorrelation p < 0.001).",
   "caveats": [
    "Consecutive years are alternating (autocorrelation -0.30), so the Mann-Kendall p-value was corrected for it (Hamed and Rao method). The t-test p-value was not corrected and may be too small."
   ]
  },
  "limitations": [
   "Values come from MERRA-2, a global reanalysis (a weather model corrected with observations), on a grid of roughly 50 km: they describe the area around the point, not the exact spot.",
   "Only straight-line trends are tested. A change that speeds up, slows down or reverses is not captured.",
   "Years that resemble the one before them make the tests over-confident. When the lag-1 autocorrelation is above 0.3 in absolute value, the verdict uses the Mann-Kendall p-value corrected for it (Hamed and Rao, 1998); the t-test p-value is never corrected.",
   "The line-up, the trend map and the emergence year use the Mann-Kendall p-value without the autocorrelation correction.",
   "A trend shows that a value changed, not why it changed."
  ]
 },
